  {
    "title": "House Democrats Continue Disregard for Safety of Pennsylvania’s Female Athletes",
    "url": "http://RepGleim.com/News/38160/Press-Releases/House-Democrats-Continue-Disregard-for-Safety-of-Pennsylvania-s-Female-Athletes",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "House Democrats Continue Disregard for Safety of Pennsylvania’s Female Athletes\nJune 17, 2026\nHARRISBURG – State Rep. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland) condemned Tuesday’s House Health Committee partisan vote to yet again re-refer\nHouse Bill 158\n,\nSenate Bill 9\nand\nHouse Bill 1849\nto the House Judiciary Committee. “This is nothing more than the latest attempt to avoid a public vote on protecting girls’ and women’s sports,” said Gleim.\nConsideration of House Bill 1849 has now been delayed for more than three months since its introduction, Senate Bill 9 has been awaiting House action for more than a year, and House Bill 158 has been stalled in committee for more than 16 months.\n“Yesterday’s actions are about delay and not policy,“ Gleim added. “Pennsylvania’s girls, parents, coaches and athletes deserve the opportunity to have their voices heard. Instead, House Democrats chose procedural gamesmanship over transparency and accountability.”\nThe re-referral is particularly troubling because both the House Health and Judiciary committees will have seen these bills twice, calling into question the sincerity of their previous statements.\n“Female athletes should not be forced to sacrifice opportunities that countless women have fought to secure,” Gleim commented.  “I will continue working to advance these bills and ensure the voices of Pennsylvania’s female athletes are not ignored.”\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Republican State Rep. Barb Gleim issued a statement criticizing the House Health Committee's vote to re-refer three bills related to transgender athlete participation in women's sports to the House Judiciary Committee. The bills, which would restrict transgender women and girls from competing in female sports categories, have been pending in committee for periods ranging from three months to over 16 months. No new legislation was introduced or passed; this release concerns a committee procedural vote.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gleim: Shapiro Slowly Bankrupting the State Using Your Tax Dollars",
    "url": "http://RepGleim.com/News/37445/Press-Releases/Gleim--Shapiro-Slowly-Bankrupting-the-State-Using-Your-Tax-Dollars",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim: Shapiro Slowly Bankrupting the State Using Your Tax Dollars\nFebruary 3, 2026\nHARRISBURG – State Rep. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland) issued the following statement in reaction to Gov. Josh Shapiro’s state budget proposal for the 2026-27 fiscal year:\n“Today I attended Gov. Josh Shapiro’s budget address and confirmed that, after 11 years of  Democrat leadership in the governor’s office, they really do think you work for them.\n“The governor proposed $53.26 billion in General Fund spending, a 5.4% increase over the current fiscal year. He wants to spend $6.8 billion more than expected revenues. Remember – when he uses phrases like investing in’ and ‘this group deserves more’ - that’s your money he’s referring to.\n“When Gov. Shapiro took office, the general fund budget was $40 billion. We’re far above that figure now. Hopefully, you can do that math because the governor surely can’t understand that he is going down an unaffordable state budget landslide into a structural deficit that you and your children will be paying for years to come.\n“He intends to pay for most of his overage with a one-time withdrawal of $4.6 billion from the Rainy Day Fund because he has to… the General Fund is completely spent down to zero.  Fortunately, this is NOT the final product. Call the governor at 717-787-2500 and tell him to spend within his means, because it’s your money.”\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Rep. Barb Gleim attended Governor Shapiro's 2026-27 budget address and issued a statement opposing the proposed $53.26 billion budget, which she says exceeds expected revenues by $6.8 billion and would draw $4.6 billion from the state's Rainy Day Fund. She criticized the budget as fiscally irresponsible and urged constituents to contact the governor's office to oppose it.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "economy",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gleim, Rothman Announce CFA Grants for Projects in Carlisle, Newville Boroughs",
    "url": "http://RepGleim.com/News/37354/Press-Releases/Gleim--Rothman-Announce-CFA-Grants-for-Projects-in-Carlisle--Newville-Boroughs",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim, Rothman Announce CFA Grants for Projects in Carlisle, Newville Boroughs\nJanuary 21, 2026\nHARRISBURG – State Sen. Greg Rothman (R-34) and Rep. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland) have secured $650,000 in Commonwealth Financing Authority (CFA) grants for Carlisle and Newville boroughs. The funding is administered through the authority’s Multimodal Transportation Fund and PA Small Water and Sewer programs.\n“Maintaining and improving infrastructure are core government functions,” Rothman said. “These roadway and water projects will benefit many residents by improving safety, service and reliability for years to come.”\n“This grant funding is essential for the health and growth of our communities,” said Gleim. “Ensuring residents have clean, reliable water and sewer services, as well as safe means of travel and transportation, are essential in every community, which is why I advocated for this funding.”\nThe CFA, an independent agency of the Pennsylvania Department of Community and Economic Development, approves competitive grants like these to fund programs and investments in Pennsylvania’s economic growth.\nThe grants recipients are:\n•\tCarlisle Borough - $400,000 for completion of a planned shared-use path along Route 74 that will allow students to walk or ride to and from the Carlisle Area School District campus.\n•\tCarlisle Borough - $150,000 for Carlisle Borough’s Basin 2 Sewer Rehabilitation Project, which will improve wastewater service for the Borough of Carlisle; and North Middleton, South Middleton, West Pennsboro, Middlesex and Silver Spring townships.\n•\tNewville Borough Water and Sewer Authority - $100,000 for purchase of a back-up generator at its water and sewer treatment plant.\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Senator Greg Rothman and Representative Barb Gleim announced that three infrastructure grants totaling $650,000 were awarded by the Commonwealth Financing Authority to Carlisle and Newville boroughs. The grants will fund a shared-use path for students along Route 74 ($400,000), sewer rehabilitation serving Carlisle and surrounding townships ($150,000), and a backup generator for Newville's water and sewer treatment plant ($100,000). These are competitive grants administered by an independent state authority, not direct appropriations by the legislators.",
      "action_type": "other",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Defending Our Daughters: Make the Promise, Says Gleim",
    "url": "http://RepGleim.com/News/37215/Press-Releases/Defending-Our-Daughters--Make-the-Promise--Says-Gleim",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Defending Our Daughters: Make the Promise, Says Gleim\nJanuary 7, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Continuing her advocacy for fairness in women’s sports, Rep. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland) invites residents of the 199th Legislative District to join the “Defending Our Daughters” campaign to show support for the Commonwealth’s female athletes.\nAll Pennsylvanians are invited to sign the “I Believe” promise to highlight the rights of girls to have a safe, fair and equal playing field; to compete to win games, championships and titles; to earn scholarships; and to have private locker rooms spaces, without males being present.\nThe promise also reinforces the shared responsibility of all Pennsylvanians, and specifically the state’s leaders, to get off the sidelines to protect and defend female athletes.\n“The genesis of the ‘Defending Our Daughters’ campaign lies in the fact that an overwhelming number of Pennsylvanians support protection for female athletes and prevention of genetic males from competing in women’s sports,” said Gleim. “The mystery lies in why House Democrat leaders are going against the will of their constituents and not giving their rank-and-file members the opportunity to vote on corresponding legislation.”\nThe “I Believe” promise is available online for people to sign at\ndefendingdaughters.com\n. Hard copies are also available in Gleim’s district office, located at 437 E North Street, Carlisle. The information will be shared with Gov. Josh Shapiro and House leaders to support action on the issue this year.\nSo far this session, majority House Democrats have avoided votes on three different bills aimed at protecting women’s sports.\nSenate Bill 9\n, which passed the Senate with bipartisan support in May, was initially – and appropriately – referred to the House Education Committee. To avoid a discharge resolution, the bill was re-referred to the Health Committee and is now in the Judiciary Committee.\nHouse Bills 158\nand\n1849\nwere also initially referred to the Education Committee but were re-referred to Judiciary in December, also to avoid discharge resolutions that would force a vote on the bills.\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Barb Gleim is promoting a public signature campaign called 'Defending Our Daughters' that asks Pennsylvanians to sign an online or paper pledge supporting legislation that would bar transgender girls and women from competing in female sports categories. She is calling attention to three bills on this topic — Senate Bill 9, House Bill 158, and House Bill 1849 — that have not received floor votes in the Democratic-controlled House, and she intends to send the collected signatures to the governor and House leadership.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "education",
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gleim, Owlett: U.S. Supreme Court Backs Basic Biology in Groundbreaking Ruling",
    "url": "http://RepGleim.com/News/38210/Press-Releases/Gleim--Owlett--U-S--Supreme-Court-Backs-Basic-Biology-in-Groundbreaking-Ruling",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim, Owlett: U.S. Supreme Court Backs Basic Biology in Groundbreaking Ruling\nJune 30, 2026\nHARRISBURG - The U.S. Supreme Court today echoed the country’s overwhelming public position that men should not be permitted to compete alongside girls in sports designated for women only. According to state Reps. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland) and Clint Owlett (R-Tioga/Bradford), the 6-3 decision and corresponding court opinion also offered an extremely important distinction – that biological sex, not “gender identity,” should be the determining and distinguishing factor for separating sports.\nThe groundbreaking ruling confirms that states have the authority and responsibility to protect female athletes. While Gov. Josh Shapiro and House Democrats constantly dodge and duck the issue, the Supreme Court is confirming that Pennsylvania’s governing athletic body, the PIAA (Pennsylvania Interscholastic Athletic Association), and local school directors should be compelled to step in and restore sanity.\n“We’re calling on the PIAA and school boards across the Commonwealth, who have not already acted to protect their girl athletes, to enact policies now,” said Owlett. “We are thankful for the nearly two dozen school districts here in Pennsylvania and the 27 states nationwide who have taken seriously protection of our girl athletes.”\nProtecting girls’ sports has long been fought for via House Republican legislation. Gleim’s original House Bill 972 of 2021, legislation that would have ensured fairness for girl athletes, passed both chambers of the General Assembly in bipartisan fashion, only to be vetoed by Democrat Gov. Tom Wolf. Several similar bills which have been introduced this session -\nSenate Bill 9\n, which passed with bipartisan support;\nHouse Bill 1849\nand\nSenate Bill 1293\n- have been summarily sidestepped by House Democrats to prevent a vote.\n“This is a momentous decision for girls and women who have fought long and hard for fairness in sports,” said Gleim. “Because of the Democrats’ inaction, the ball is now in the PIAA’s court. It’s finally time to reinstitute fairness, protect our girl athletes, and affirm biological reality.”\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Pennsylvania state Representatives Barb Gleim and Clint Owlett issued a statement responding to a U.S. Supreme Court ruling on transgender athlete participation in sports, calling on the PIAA and local school boards to adopt policies restricting transgender girls from competing in girls' sports. They highlighted previously introduced legislation on the topic, including bills that passed one or both chambers but were vetoed or blocked from a floor vote.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "education",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
