  {
    "title": "Gleim Calls for State Compliance in Federal Stormwater Policy",
    "url": "http://RepGleim.com/News/38802/Press-Releases/Gleim-Calls-for-State-Compliance-in-Federal-Stormwater-Policy",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim Calls for State Compliance in Federal Stormwater Policy\nSeptember 23, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Following the federal government’s action to rescind\nExecutive Order 13508, Chesapeake Bay Protection and Restoration\n, Rep. Barbara Gleim (R-Cumberland) is calling on the Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection (DEP) and the Shapiro administration to immediately halt and review Chesapeake Bay-related stormwater mandates.\n“After 16 years, and U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) stormwater guidelines in Pennsylvania that don’t mirror Washington, the federal government changed course and Harrisburg needs to as well,” Gleim stated. “The federal Municipal Separate Storm Sewer System (MS4) program existed before the 2009 executive order; however, the years following Executive Order 13508 brought a significant expansion of Chesapeake Bay-specific pollution-reduction policies and compliance requirements.”\nIn 2010, the EPA established the Chesapeake Bay Total Maximum Daily Load (TMDL), setting pollution limits for nitrogen, phosphorus and sediment. Pennsylvania subsequently developed Watershed Implementation Plans to meet those targets, and Chesapeake Bay pollutant-reduction requirements were incorporated into the state’s MS4 permitting and compliance process.\nOver time, municipalities were required to develop pollutant-reduction plans, calculate pollutant loads, identify projects and best management practices and meet specific reduction targets, which created substantial engineering, construction, reporting and compliance costs. For municipalities and property owners, those costs contribute to stormwater charges commonly referred to by residents as a “rain tax.”\nKnowing that other states were not mandating fees or taxes, but just giving guidance, Gleim set out to prove that the stream modeling the state was using was faulty, and thus, so was the permitting and compliance process.  She introduced House Bill 207 in 2023 and House Bill 958 in the current session, requiring the state to test the waterways and update the modeling, as to whether the stream was impaired or not, so municipalities were not fixing something that had already been remediated.\nThe modeling and assumptions in the state system need to accurately reflect conditions in Pennsylvania waterways.\n“I was able to convince North Middleton Township that streams they were attempting to repair were not impaired using updated stream analysis performed locally,” added Gleim. “The tax they were imposing and liens on homes for those who couldn’t pay was unjust and burdensome, to say the least, especially for the elderly. I’m glad they eliminated it.”\nSince 2019, York County has worked with the U.S. Geological Survey to conduct extensive in-stream monitoring in six major watersheds. The program measures actual nitrogen, phosphorus, and suspended sediment concentrations and loads, providing real-world data that can be compared with the modeled estimates used in Chesapeake Bay planning. Gleim is calling on DEP to follow this best practice model throughout the entire state.\n“Before we require municipalities and property owners to spend millions of dollars, we should compare the computer models against what we are measuring in the water,” Gleim commented.\r\n“If the model says one thing and actual water samples say another, we should follow the science and the actual measurements. Taxpayers should not be charged based on an assumption when we can test the water and determine what is there.”\nFurthermore, municipalities and residents were also made aware that certain cities that dump into the Susquehanna, like the city of Harrisburg, were conveniently exempt from the stormwater mandates. According to Gleim, this type of exemption prevents cleanup of the Chesapeake Bay, and serves as a revenue source for some cities and municipalities, engineers and DEP.\nGleim agrees that Pennsylvania now has an obligation to separate what remains legally required from requirements that were imposed or expanded as part of the Chesapeake Bay strategy that followed Executive Order 13508.\n“DEP should change course immediately because of the change in federal policy. Pennsylvania must now identify what the law requires, what was added to satisfy Chesapeake Bay policy and pollution-reduction goals and what Pennsylvania has the authority to change or eliminate,” Gleim said.\nGleim agrees with her colleague, Rep. Breanda Pugh (R-Luzerne) who testified the following at a recent hearing:\n“You cannot call something a statewide environmental priority and then send the bill only to selected communities. If Pennsylvania decides these projects are necessary for the public good, then Pennsylvania should help pay for them.\n“Our municipalities and residents should not be singled out to finance a benefit that extends far beyond their municipal boundaries.”\nTo highlight exemptions, Gleim also introduced House Bills 234 and 235 four years ago, which would have allowed farm exemptions from the MS4 tax (something the Pennsylvania Farm Bureau supports).\n“The very businesses that are actually absorbing the water, and in some cases, mitigating water runoff, should not be taxed or forced to mitigate, when they are doing the very thing the EPA and the state are requiring,” Gleim added. “The requirements, again, were not implemented the same across the state.\n“This is particularly important to the residents of the 199th Legislative District and communities across Cumberland County,” Gleim said. Our residents have been forced to shoulder enormous costs associated with mandates they never voted for and had virtually no control over.\n“I am grateful to EPA Administrator Lee Zeldin for making the well-being of our residents a priority, and for U.S. Small Business Association Administrator Kelly Loeffler and her staff for listening to the needs of small businesses in a meeting in Lancaster County with farmers last year.”\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Barbara Gleim issued a statement calling on the Pennsylvania DEP and Shapiro administration to halt and review Chesapeake Bay stormwater mandates following the federal rescission of Executive Order 13508. She referenced two bills she introduced (HB 207 in 2023 and HB 958 in the current session) that would require the state to update its waterway modeling before imposing compliance costs on municipalities. She also called on DEP to adopt real-time in-stream water monitoring, similar to a York County program, rather than relying on computer models to determine pollution levels.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "environment",
        "infrastructure",
        "economy"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gleim’s Advocacy Silenced by Democrat Leadership on Women in Sports Bill",
    "url": "http://RepGleim.com/News/38842/Press-Releases/Gleim-s-Advocacy-Silenced-by-Democrat-Leadership-on-Women-in-Sports-Bill",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim’s Advocacy Silenced by Democrat Leadership on Women in Sports Bill\nSeptember 29, 2026\nHARRISBURG - For the 16th time this session, House Democrat leadership has instructed a committee chairmen to rerefer a piece of legislation designed to prevent men from participating in women’s sports. In addition, Speaker of the House Joanna McClinton refused to recognize a request made by Rep. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland), who wanted to announce a discharge resolution on\nSenate Bill 1293\n, which would require schools to designate athletic teams as male, female or co-ed.\n“Women and girls deserve fair athletic competition and House members deserve the opportunity to vote on this legislation,” Gleim said. “I wanted to file the discharge resolution so the voices of 8 out of 10 Pennsylvanians would be heard; however, those voices can’t be represented when House rules are violated and committee chairs pass around legislation for two years.”\nThe House Democrat majority has repeatedly declined to allow women’s sports legislation to be considered in the House chamber.\nSenate Bill 9\nand\nHouse Bills 158\nand\n1849\nhave each been moved repeatedly among House committees without a final vote being taken. All four bills now reside in the Health Committee after two years of rereferrals.\nGleim’s discharge resolution would have begun the process of removing Senate Bill 1293 from committee and bringing it before the full House for a vote. Instead, House Education Committee Chairman Pete Schweyer chose to bring the bill up in committee and again rerefer it with no explanation.\n“This latest ‘episode’ is even more egregious because the committee chairman refused to allow full discussion prior to the vote to rerefer the bill,” added Gleim. “We are sent here to speak on behalf of our constituents, whose overwhelming desire to keep men out of women’s sports in Pennsylvania continues to be muted.”\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Barb Gleim attempted to file a discharge resolution to bring Senate Bill 1293, which would require schools to designate athletic teams as male, female, or co-ed, to a full House vote. Speaker McClinton declined to recognize her request, and the House Education Committee chair instead moved the bill to another committee without debate. This is the 16th time this session that similar legislation has been rereferred in committee rather than receiving a floor vote.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "education",
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
