  {
    "title": "Gleim’s Advocacy Silenced by Democrat Leadership on Women in Sports Bill",
    "url": "https://www.repgleim.com/News/38842/Press-Releases/Gleim-s-Advocacy-Silenced-by-Democrat-Leadership-on-Women-in-Sports-Bill",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim’s Advocacy Silenced by Democrat Leadership on Women in Sports Bill\nSeptember 29, 2026\nHARRISBURG - For the 16th time this session, House Democrat leadership has instructed a committee chairmen to rerefer a piece of legislation designed to prevent men from participating in women’s sports. In addition, Speaker of the House Joanna McClinton refused to recognize a request made by Rep. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland), who wanted to announce a discharge resolution on\nSenate Bill 1293\n, which would require schools to designate athletic teams as male, female or co-ed.\n“Women and girls deserve fair athletic competition and House members deserve the opportunity to vote on this legislation,” Gleim said. “I wanted to file the discharge resolution so the voices of 8 out of 10 Pennsylvanians would be heard; however, those voices can’t be represented when House rules are violated and committee chairs pass around legislation for two years.”\nThe House Democrat majority has repeatedly declined to allow women’s sports legislation to be considered in the House chamber.\nSenate Bill 9\nand\nHouse Bills 158\nand\n1849\nhave each been moved repeatedly among House committees without a final vote being taken. All four bills now reside in the Health Committee after two years of rereferrals.\nGleim’s discharge resolution would have begun the process of removing Senate Bill 1293 from committee and bringing it before the full House for a vote. Instead, House Education Committee Chairman Pete Schweyer chose to bring the bill up in committee and again rerefer it with no explanation.\n“This latest ‘episode’ is even more egregious because the committee chairman refused to allow full discussion prior to the vote to rerefer the bill,” added Gleim. “We are sent here to speak on behalf of our constituents, whose overwhelming desire to keep men out of women’s sports in Pennsylvania continues to be muted.”\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Barb Gleim attempted to file a discharge resolution to bring Senate Bill 1293, which would require schools to designate athletic teams as male, female, or co-ed, to a full House floor vote. Speaker McClinton declined to recognize her request, and the House Education Committee chair instead moved the bill to yet another committee without discussion. This is the 16th time this session that similar legislation has been rereferraled in committee rather than receiving a full House vote.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "education",
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gleim Calls for State Compliance in Federal Stormwater Policy",
    "url": "https://www.repgleim.com/News/38802/Press-Releases/Gleim-Calls-for-State-Compliance-in-Federal-Stormwater-Policy",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim Calls for State Compliance in Federal Stormwater Policy\nSeptember 23, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Following the federal government’s action to rescind\nExecutive Order 13508, Chesapeake Bay Protection and Restoration\n, Rep. Barbara Gleim (R-Cumberland) is calling on the Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection (DEP) and the Shapiro administration to immediately halt and review Chesapeake Bay-related stormwater mandates.\n“After 16 years, and U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) stormwater guidelines in Pennsylvania that don’t mirror Washington, the federal government changed course and Harrisburg needs to as well,” Gleim stated. “The federal Municipal Separate Storm Sewer System (MS4) program existed before the 2009 executive order; however, the years following Executive Order 13508 brought a significant expansion of Chesapeake Bay-specific pollution-reduction policies and compliance requirements.”\nIn 2010, the EPA established the Chesapeake Bay Total Maximum Daily Load (TMDL), setting pollution limits for nitrogen, phosphorus and sediment. Pennsylvania subsequently developed Watershed Implementation Plans to meet those targets, and Chesapeake Bay pollutant-reduction requirements were incorporated into the state’s MS4 permitting and compliance process.\nOver time, municipalities were required to develop pollutant-reduction plans, calculate pollutant loads, identify projects and best management practices and meet specific reduction targets, which created substantial engineering, construction, reporting and compliance costs. For municipalities and property owners, those costs contribute to stormwater charges commonly referred to by residents as a “rain tax.”\nKnowing that other states were not mandating fees or taxes, but just giving guidance, Gleim set out to prove that the stream modeling the state was using was faulty, and thus, so was the permitting and compliance process.  She introduced House Bill 207 in 2023 and House Bill 958 in the current session, requiring the state to test the waterways and update the modeling, as to whether the stream was impaired or not, so municipalities were not fixing something that had already been remediated.\nThe modeling and assumptions in the state system need to accurately reflect conditions in Pennsylvania waterways.\n“I was able to convince North Middleton Township that streams they were attempting to repair were not impaired using updated stream analysis performed locally,” added Gleim. “The tax they were imposing and liens on homes for those who couldn’t pay was unjust and burdensome, to say the least, especially for the elderly. I’m glad they eliminated it.”\nSince 2019, York County has worked with the U.S. Geological Survey to conduct extensive in-stream monitoring in six major watersheds. The program measures actual nitrogen, phosphorus, and suspended sediment concentrations and loads, providing real-world data that can be compared with the modeled estimates used in Chesapeake Bay planning. Gleim is calling on DEP to follow this best practice model throughout the entire state.\n“Before we require municipalities and property owners to spend millions of dollars, we should compare the computer models against what we are measuring in the water,” Gleim commented.\r\n“If the model says one thing and actual water samples say another, we should follow the science and the actual measurements. Taxpayers should not be charged based on an assumption when we can test the water and determine what is there.”\nFurthermore, municipalities and residents were also made aware that certain cities that dump into the Susquehanna, like the city of Harrisburg, were conveniently exempt from the stormwater mandates. According to Gleim, this type of exemption prevents cleanup of the Chesapeake Bay, and serves as a revenue source for some cities and municipalities, engineers and DEP.\nGleim agrees that Pennsylvania now has an obligation to separate what remains legally required from requirements that were imposed or expanded as part of the Chesapeake Bay strategy that followed Executive Order 13508.\n“DEP should change course immediately because of the change in federal policy. Pennsylvania must now identify what the law requires, what was added to satisfy Chesapeake Bay policy and pollution-reduction goals and what Pennsylvania has the authority to change or eliminate,” Gleim said.\nGleim agrees with her colleague, Rep. Breanda Pugh (R-Luzerne) who testified the following at a recent hearing:\n“You cannot call something a statewide environmental priority and then send the bill only to selected communities. If Pennsylvania decides these projects are necessary for the public good, then Pennsylvania should help pay for them.\n“Our municipalities and residents should not be singled out to finance a benefit that extends far beyond their municipal boundaries.”\nTo highlight exemptions, Gleim also introduced House Bills 234 and 235 four years ago, which would have allowed farm exemptions from the MS4 tax (something the Pennsylvania Farm Bureau supports).\n“The very businesses that are actually absorbing the water, and in some cases, mitigating water runoff, should not be taxed or forced to mitigate, when they are doing the very thing the EPA and the state are requiring,” Gleim added. “The requirements, again, were not implemented the same across the state.\n“This is particularly important to the residents of the 199th Legislative District and communities across Cumberland County,” Gleim said. Our residents have been forced to shoulder enormous costs associated with mandates they never voted for and had virtually no control over.\n“I am grateful to EPA Administrator Lee Zeldin for making the well-being of our residents a priority, and for U.S. Small Business Association Administrator Kelly Loeffler and her staff for listening to the needs of small businesses in a meeting in Lancaster County with farmers last year.”\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Barbara Gleim issued a statement calling on Pennsylvania's DEP and the Shapiro administration to pause and review Chesapeake Bay-related stormwater regulations following the federal rescission of Executive Order 13508. She referenced two bills she introduced (HB 207 in 2023 and HB 958 in the current session) that would require the state to update its stream modeling before imposing compliance costs on municipalities. She also called on DEP to adopt real-time in-stream water monitoring, similar to a program already operating in York County, rather than relying on computer models to determine pollution levels.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "environment",
        "infrastructure",
        "economy"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gleim, Rothman Announce More Than $1 Million in LSA Funding for Local Projects",
    "url": "https://www.repgleim.com/News/38733/Press-Releases/Gleim--Rothman-Announce-More-Than--1-Million-in-LSA-Funding-for-Local-Projects",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim, Rothman Announce More Than $1 Million in LSA Funding for Local Projects\nSeptember 15, 2026\nHARRISBURG – State Sen. Greg Rothman (R-34) and Rep. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland) are announcing more than $1 million in Local Share Account (LSA) funding that is being awarded to two projects in the 199th Legislative District.\n“These investments will have a direct and meaningful impact on our community,” said Gleim. “From ensuring our first responders have the equipment they need to protect public safety, to supporting an organization that provides valuable services to residents, these projects are worthy investments in the future of our area. I am pleased to have supported these funding requests and am grateful to see that they received approval.”\nThe West Pennsboro Township Fire Company plans to purchase a new mini pumper fire truck with the more than $427,000 grant it is receiving. Also, a $600,000 grant is being awarded to New Life Community Church for instillation of a new HVAC system that will allow it to better serve community groups.\n“I was pleased to advocate for this grant funding to keep our community safe and well supported,” Rothman added. “These state investments equip our local first responders and help ensure important needs are met within our community.”\nThe Local Share Account (LSA) program provides funding for projects that promote economic development, community improvement and public interest projects across Pennsylvania.\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nSenator Greg Rothman\n34th Legislative District\nPennsylvania State Senate\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Pennsylvania state legislators Barb Gleim and Greg Rothman announced that two local organizations in Cumberland County will receive over $1 million in state Local Share Account grants. West Pennsboro Township Fire Company will receive $427,000 for a new fire truck, and New Life Community Church will receive $600,000 for a new HVAC system. The legislators advocated for these grants, which were approved through the state's LSA program.",
      "action_type": "other",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "economy",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gleim Announces $1 Million RACP Funding Recipient in Middlesex Township",
    "url": "https://www.repgleim.com/News/38343/Press-Releases/Gleim-Announces--1-Million-RACP-Funding-Recipient-in-Middlesex-Township",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim Announces $1 Million RACP Funding Recipient in Middlesex Township\nJuly 17, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– Rep. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland) announced today that Carlisle Community Improvements has been awarded a $1 million Redevelopment Assistance Capital Program (RACP) grant to support the renovation and expansion of the Carlisle Country Club clubhouse.\nThe funding will help advance the project by covering both design and construction costs associated with the clubhouse improvements. Grant funds will be used for concrete, carpentry, drywall, HVAC, plumbing, sprinkler, finishes, electrical work, and windows and doors, helping bring the project to completion.\n“This investment is great news for our community,” Gleim said. “The Carlisle Country Club has long been an important part of Cumberland County, and these improvements will enhance the facility, while strengthening the area’s ability to host events and attract visitors. I am pleased to see state funding used to move this project forward.”\nRACP provides financial assistance for the acquisition and construction of regional, economic, recreational, and historical improvement projects that generate lasting benefits for Pennsylvania communities.\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Barb Gleim announced that Carlisle Community Improvements received a $1 million state RACP grant to renovate and expand the Carlisle Country Club clubhouse in Middlesex Township. The grant will fund construction work including HVAC, plumbing, electrical, and other building improvements. Gleim's role was announcing the award, not directly securing or voting on it.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "economy",
        "infrastructure",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Gleim, Owlett: U.S. Supreme Court Backs Basic Biology in Groundbreaking Ruling",
    "url": "https://www.repgleim.com/News/38210/Press-Releases/Gleim--Owlett--U-S--Supreme-Court-Backs-Basic-Biology-in-Groundbreaking-Ruling",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Gleim, Owlett: U.S. Supreme Court Backs Basic Biology in Groundbreaking Ruling\nJune 30, 2026\nHARRISBURG - The U.S. Supreme Court today echoed the country’s overwhelming public position that men should not be permitted to compete alongside girls in sports designated for women only. According to state Reps. Barb Gleim (R-Cumberland) and Clint Owlett (R-Tioga/Bradford), the 6-3 decision and corresponding court opinion also offered an extremely important distinction – that biological sex, not “gender identity,” should be the determining and distinguishing factor for separating sports.\nThe groundbreaking ruling confirms that states have the authority and responsibility to protect female athletes. While Gov. Josh Shapiro and House Democrats constantly dodge and duck the issue, the Supreme Court is confirming that Pennsylvania’s governing athletic body, the PIAA (Pennsylvania Interscholastic Athletic Association), and local school directors should be compelled to step in and restore sanity.\n“We’re calling on the PIAA and school boards across the Commonwealth, who have not already acted to protect their girl athletes, to enact policies now,” said Owlett. “We are thankful for the nearly two dozen school districts here in Pennsylvania and the 27 states nationwide who have taken seriously protection of our girl athletes.”\nProtecting girls’ sports has long been fought for via House Republican legislation. Gleim’s original House Bill 972 of 2021, legislation that would have ensured fairness for girl athletes, passed both chambers of the General Assembly in bipartisan fashion, only to be vetoed by Democrat Gov. Tom Wolf. Several similar bills which have been introduced this session -\nSenate Bill 9\n, which passed with bipartisan support;\nHouse Bill 1849\nand\nSenate Bill 1293\n- have been summarily sidestepped by House Democrats to prevent a vote.\n“This is a momentous decision for girls and women who have fought long and hard for fairness in sports,” said Gleim. “Because of the Democrats’ inaction, the ball is now in the PIAA’s court. It’s finally time to reinstitute fairness, protect our girl athletes, and affirm biological reality.”\nRepresentative Barb Gleim\n199th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Scott Little\n717-260-6137\nslittle@pahousegop.com\nRepGleim.com / Facebook.com/RepGleim\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Pennsylvania state Representatives Barb Gleim and Clint Owlett issued a statement responding to a 6-3 U.S. Supreme Court ruling they say affirms states' authority to restrict transgender athletes from competing in female sports categories. They called on the PIAA and local school boards to enact policies limiting athletic competition to biological sex. They also highlighted several pending Pennsylvania bills on the issue that have not received floor votes.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
